{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":211447,"duration_api_ms":208481,"num_turns":19,"result":"## COMPLETE WDD YAML FILE\n\nBelow is the complete Workflow Definition Document for the 1000 Genomes workflow:\n\n```yaml\nschema_version: wrd-7.0\nmetadata:\n  workflow_lineage_id: wrd:1000genome\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  version_notes: \"Initial WDD compiled from daxgen.py Pegasus workflow definition\"\n  deprecated: false\n  workflow_name: \"1000 Genomes\"\n  workflow_description: >\n    The 1000 Genomes workflow identifies mutational overlaps in human genetic variation\n    data from the 1000 Genomes Project. It fetches, parses, and analyzes genotype data\n    across 26 populations, computing SNP overlaps between individuals and filtering by\n    predicted functional impact scores (SIFT). The workflow processes chromosome-level\n    VCF files, extracts per-individual mutation sets, filters for functional variants,\n    and generates statistical null distributions of mutation overlaps across population\n    subsets. Results include per-population mutation overlap matrices and frequency\n    distributions used for disease-association studies.\n  source_repository: \"https://github.com/rosafilgueira/Mutation_Sets\"\n  source_commit: null\n  source_format: compiled_from_pegasus_dax\n  source_file: \"daxgen.py\"\n  generated_by: claude\n  generated_at: \"2026-03-11T00:00:00Z\"\n  last_modified_at: \"2026-03-11T00:00:00Z\"\n\nstages:\n  - stage_id: stage:parsing\n    name: \"Parsing and Filtering\"\n    order: 1\n    description: >\n      Parses chromosome VCF files and extracts per-individual mutation data.\n      Individuals task parallelizes by chromosome chunks; individuals_merge consolidates.\n    tasks:\n      - task:individuals\n      - task:individuals_merge\n\n  - stage_id: stage:annotation\n    name: \"SIFT Annotation\"\n    order: 2\n    description: >\n      Filters VEP annotation files to extract SIFT functional impact scores\n      for SNP variants, producing a deduplicated reference file per chromosome.\n    tasks:\n      - task:sifting\n\n  - stage_id: stage:analysis\n    name: \"Mutation Overlap Analysis\"\n    order: 3\n    description: >\n      Computes pairwise mutation overlaps between individuals and frequency\n      distributions, stratified by population and chromosome. Produces statistical\n      null distributions and summary statistics.\n    tasks:\n      - task:mutation_overlap\n      - task:frequency\n\ntasks:\n  - task_id: task:individuals\n    name: \"Individuals\"\n    stage: stage:parsing\n    executable: \"bin/individuals.py\"\n    description: >\n      Parses a chunk of a chromosome VCF file and extracts the genotypes of each\n      individual at variant sites. Reads a contiguous range of lines from the input\n      VCF file (specified by counter/stop parameters), filters for SNP alleles\n      (AF threshold 0.5), and writes one output file per individual containing\n      mutation records with position, reference, alternate, and AF metadata.\n      Outputs are compressed into a single tar.gz archive. This task is instantiated\n      once per chromosome chunk, enabling parallel processing across the genome.\n    behavioral_notes: >\n      Streaming I/O: reads input VCF file once, sequentially through the specified\n      line range. Incremental writes: creates one output file per individual (2504\n      individuals total, but subset by population/chunk), writing line-by-line as\n      mutations are identified. Tar archive creation at task completion. Memory\n      pattern: holds all processed mutations for each individual in memory before\n      writing. No internal checkpointing; all-or-nothing completion semantics.\n    parallelism_model:\n      type: embarrassingly_parallel\n      description: >\n        Each individuals job processes a disjoint range of VCF lines for one chromosome.\n        The number of jobs per chromosome is configurable via the ind_jobs parameter\n        (default 1, up to 250 for 250k-line VCF files). Jobs are independent and can\n        execute concurrently.\n    io_dominance: io_bound\n    io_access_type: python_serial\n    restart_model: idempotent\n\n  - task_id: task:individuals_merge\n    name: \"Individuals Merge\"\n    stage: stage:parsing\n    executable: \"bin/individuals_merge.py\"\n    description: >\n      Consolidates multiple tar.gz archives produced by parallel individuals jobs\n      for a single chromosome. Extracts all per-individual files from each input\n      archive, merges duplicate filenames (concatenating contents), and re-archives\n      into a single consolidated tar.gz. This task runs once per chromosome after\n      all individuals jobs complete, producing a single merged output file.\n    behavioral_notes: >\n      Tar archive extraction at startup, in-memory file list merging, tar archive\n      creation at completion. Reads all input archives fully; writes complete merged\n      archive. Uses temporary directories for extraction. Linear memory with respect\n      to total unique individuals across all input chunks. No internal iteration;\n      single pass merge.\n    parallelism_model:\n      type: serial\n      description: >\n        One merge job per chromosome, executing after all individuals jobs for that\n        chromosome complete. Dependencies enforced implicitly through file availability.\n    io_dominance: io_bound\n    io_access_type: python_serial\n    restart_model: idempotent\n\n  - task_id: task:sifting\n    name: \"SIFTING\"\n    stage: stage:annotation\n    executable: \"bin/sifting.py\"\n    description: >\n      Processes VEP (Variant Effect Predictor) annotation data for a chromosome,\n      filtering for SNP variants with SIFT functional impact predictions.\n      Extracts SIFT scores, ENSEMBL gene IDs, and HGNC gene names from the VEP\n      file. Outputs a deduplicated reference file containing rs number, ENSEMBL ID,\n      SIFT impact category, and phenotype prediction, one per variant. Used\n      downstream by mutation_overlap and frequency analyses for functional annotation.\n    behavioral_notes: >\n      Full file read at startup (using subprocess grep for initial filtering).\n      Sequential line-by-line parsing of filtered results. Single-pass output write.\n      Temporary intermediate file created (SIFT.chr*.vcf) and deleted after processing.\n      Memory usage bounded by grep output size; no accumulation of full VEP file.\n    parallelism_model:\n      type: serial\n      description: >\n        One sifting job per chromosome, independent from individuals/merge pipeline.\n        Can execute in parallel with individuals jobs for the same or different chromosomes.\n    io_dominance: io_bound\n    io_access_type: python_serial\n    restart_model: idempotent\n\n  - task_id: task:mutation_overlap\n    name: \"Mutation Overlap\"\n    stage: stage:analysis\n    executable: \"bin/mutation_overlap.py\"\n    description: >\n      Computes pairwise mutation overlaps between individuals for a single\n      chromosome-population pair. Reads the merged individuals tar.gz, extracts\n      per-individual mutation files, reads the sifted VEP reference, and the\n      population membership list. Computes a mutation overlap matrix where each\n      entry is the count of shared mutations between two individuals, optionally\n      filtered by SIFT score. Generates summary statistics and PNG plots of\n      overlap distributions (heatmaps, histograms). Outputs results as tar.gz archive.\n    behavioral_notes: >\n      Tar archive extraction to working directory at startup. Full read of population\n      and columns metadata files. Incremental matrix computation (accumulates pairwise\n      counts). Matplotlib figure generation and PNG output at completion (memory-intensive).\n      All computation in-memory using numpy arrays. No streaming of overlap matrix\n      computation; requires all individual data to be memory-resident. Produces multiple\n      PNG output files written sequentially.\n    parallelism_model:\n      type: embarrassingly_parallel\n      description: >\n        Instantiated as a Cartesian product: (number of chromosomes) × (number of\n        populations). Each job is independent, processing one chromosome-population pair.\n        Typically 10-22 chromosomes × 7 populations (ALL, EUR, EAS, AFR, AMR, SAS, GBR),\n        resulting in 70-154 jobs. All can execute concurrently.\n    io_dominance: io_bound\n    io_access_type: python_serial\n    restart_model: idempotent\n\n  - task_id: task:frequency\n    name: \"Frequency\"\n    stage: stage:analysis\n    executable: \"bin/frequency.py\"\n    description: >\n      Computes frequency distributions of mutation overlaps across random samples\n      of individuals for a single chromosome-population pair. Similar to mutation_overlap\n      but performs Monte Carlo sampling: selects n_runs random samples of n_indiv\n      individuals, computes mutation overlaps for each sample, and accumulates\n      frequency distributions. Generates NULL distribution of random mutation overlap\n      counts for statistical significance testing of disease-associated variants.\n      Outputs results and plots as tar.gz archive.\n    behavioral_notes: >\n      Tar archive extraction at startup. Random sampling with seed (reproducible).\n      Incremental frequency histogram accumulation over n_runs iterations.\n      Each iteration: samples individuals, computes overlap matrix, updates histogram.\n      Matplotlib output and PNG generation at completion. All computation in-memory.\n      High memory usage due to multiple overlap matrix instances during iteration.\n      No checkpointing; requires full re-execution if interrupted.\n    parallelism_model:\n      type: embarrassingly_parallel\n      description: >\n        Instantiated as a Cartesian product: (number of chromosomes) × (number of\n        populations). Same cardinality as mutation_overlap (~70-154 jobs).\n        Independent execution, concurrent scheduling.\n    io_dominance: compute_bound\n    io_access_type: python_serial\n    restart_model: idempotent\n\ndata_objects:\n  - data_id: data:vcf_input\n    name: \"VCF Chromosome Input\"\n    format: \"VCF (Variant Call Format)\"\n    cardinality: \"per chromosome (1 file per chromosome, 1-22 or 1-ALL)\"\n    source: \"1000 Genomes Phase 3 Release\"\n    lifecycle_stage: \"input\"\n    size_hint: \"250,000 lines per chromosome (6-7 GB uncompressed)\"\n    notes: >\n      Input VCF files from 1000 Genomes Project Phase3, one per chromosome.\n      Contains all SNP and small indel variants for 2504 individuals.\n      Read by individuals task; line range specified by counter/stop parameters.\n\n  - data_id: data:vep_annotation\n    name: \"VEP Annotation File\"\n    format: \"VCF (Variant Call Format) with VEP consequence annotations\"\n    cardinality: \"per chromosome\"\n    source: \"VEP Pipeline (Ensembl)\"\n    lifecycle_stage: \"input\"\n    size_hint: \"Large (500MB-2GB per chromosome)\"\n    notes: >\n      Variant Effect Predictor output annotating each variant with functional\n      predictions (SIFT score, ENSEMBL gene ID, HGNC symbol, phenotype).\n      Read by sifting task; filtered output is read by mutation_overlap and frequency.\n\n  - data_id: data:population_list\n    name: \"Population Membership File\"\n    format: \"Plain text, whitespace-delimited individual IDs\"\n    cardinality: \"per population (7 total: ALL, EUR, EAS, AFR, AMR, SAS, GBR)\"\n    source: \"1000 Genomes Metadata\"\n    lifecycle_stage: \"input\"\n    size_hint: \"~1-50 KB per file (depends on population size)\"\n    notes: >\n      Lists individual IDs belonging to each population/superpopulation.\n      ALL population includes all 2504 individuals; others are subsets.\n      Read by mutation_overlap and frequency tasks to stratify analysis.\n\n  - data_id: data:columns_metadata\n    name: \"Sample Columns Metadata\"\n    format: \"Tab-delimited text\"\n    cardinality: \"single, constant\"\n    source: \"1000 Genomes Metadata\"\n    lifecycle_stage: \"input\"\n    size_hint: \"~50-100 KB\"\n    notes: >\n      Maps VCF column indices to individual IDs. Used by individuals task to\n      name output files and by mutation_overlap/frequency to align with population\n      lists.\n\n  - data_id: data:individuals_chunk\n    name: \"Individuals Chunk Archive\"\n    format: \"tar.gz archive of text files\"\n    cardinality: \"multiple per chromosome (equal to ind_jobs parameter)\"\n    producer: task:individuals\n    consumer: task:individuals_merge\n    lifecycle_stage: \"intermediate\"\n    size_hint: \"50-500 MB per chunk (depends on chunk size)\"\n    notes: >\n      Per-individual mutation files extracted from VCF chunk, one file per individual\n      in the chunk. Archived and compressed. Temporary; merged and discarded after\n      individuals_merge completes.\n\n  - data_id: data:individuals_merged\n    name: \"Merged Individuals Archive\"\n    format: \"tar.gz archive of text files\"\n    cardinality: \"per chromosome (equal to number of chromosomes in analysis)\"\n    producer: task:individuals_merge\n    consumer: task:mutation_overlap, task:frequency\n    lifecycle_stage: \"intermediate\"\n    size_hint: \"500 MB - 5 GB per chromosome\"\n    notes: >\n      Consolidated per-individual mutation files for one chromosome, merged from\n      all chunk archives. Contains one file per individual (subset by population\n      in downstream analysis). Re-used by multiple downstream jobs (mutation_overlap\n      and frequency for all populations).\n\n  - data_id: data:sifted_reference\n    name: \"SIFT-Filtered Reference\"\n    format: \"Plain text, space/tab-delimited\"\n    cardinality: \"per chromosome\"\n    producer: task:sifting\n    consumer: task:mutation_overlap, task:frequency\n    lifecycle_stage: \"intermediate\"\n    size_hint: \"10-100 MB per chromosome\"\n    notes: >\n      Filtered variant reference: rs number, ENSEMBL gene ID, SIFT score, phenotype.\n      One file per chromosome. Read by mutation_overlap and frequency for all\n      population pairs. Re-used across multiple downstream jobs.\n\n  - data_id: data:mutation_overlap_output\n    name: \"Mutation Overlap Results\"\n    format: \"tar.gz archive containing: overlap matrix CSV, summary statistics text, PNG plots\"\n    cardinality: \"per (chromosome, population) pair\"\n    producer: task:mutation_overlap\n    lifecycle_stage: \"output\"\n    size_hint: \"5-100 MB per archive (depends on population size)\"\n    notes: >\n      Per-population mutation overlap matrix (pairwise overlap counts between individuals),\n      summary statistics, and matplotlib plots (heatmaps, histograms). Staged out to\n      output storage. Final deliverable.\n\n  - data_id: data:frequency_output\n    name: \"Frequency Distribution Results\"\n    format: \"tar.gz archive containing: frequency histograms CSV, summary statistics, PNG plots\"\n    cardinality: \"per (chromosome, population) pair\"\n    producer: task:frequency\n    lifecycle_stage: \"output\"\n    size_hint: \"5-100 MB per archive\"\n    notes: >\n      Null distribution of random mutation overlap frequencies (Monte Carlo sampling),\n      summary statistics, and matplotlib plots. One per population per chromosome.\n      Staged out to output storage. Final deliverable.\n\nproducer_consumer_edges:\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:vcf->individuals\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:individuals\n    data_id: data:vcf_input\n    cardinality: n_to_1\n    pattern: many_chunk_inputs_per_task\n    notes: >\n      One VCF file read by all individuals jobs for that chromosome, each processing\n      a disjoint line range. External input stage-in required.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:individuals->individuals_merge\n    producer_task: task:individuals\n    consumer_task: task:individuals_merge\n    data_id: data:individuals_chunk\n    cardinality: n_to_1\n    pattern: gather\n    notes: >\n      Multiple individuals chunks (per ind_jobs parameter) produced, all consumed\n      by a single merge job per chromosome. Strong data dependency: merge cannot\n      start until all chunks complete. Temporary files; can be deleted after merge.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:individuals_merge->mutation_overlap\n    producer_task: task:individuals_merge\n    consumer_task: task:mutation_overlap\n    data_id: data:individuals_merged\n    cardinality: 1_to_many\n    pattern: scatter\n    notes: >\n      Single merged individuals archive per chromosome consumed by multiple downstream\n      jobs (one per population). File re-use: each mutation_overlap job extracts and\n      processes independently.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:individuals_merge->frequency\n    producer_task: task:individuals_merge\n    consumer_task: task:frequency\n    data_id: data:individuals_merged\n    cardinality: 1_to_many\n    pattern: scatter\n    notes: >\n      Single merged individuals archive per chromosome consumed by multiple frequency\n      jobs (one per population). Same re-use pattern as mutation_overlap.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:vep->sifting\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:sifting\n    data_id: data:vep_annotation\n    cardinality: 1_to_1\n    pattern: direct\n    notes: >\n      One VEP file per chromosome read by sifting task. External input, independent\n      of individuals pipeline.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:sifting->mutation_overlap\n    producer_task: task:sifting\n    consumer_task: task:mutation_overlap\n    data_id: data:sifted_reference\n    cardinality: 1_to_many\n    pattern: scatter\n    notes: >\n      Single sifted reference per chromosome consumed by all mutation_overlap jobs\n      for that chromosome (one per population). File re-use across multiple analysis jobs.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:sifting->frequency\n    producer_task: task:sifting\n    consumer_task: task:frequency\n    data_id: data:sifted_reference\n    cardinality: 1_to_many\n    pattern: scatter\n    notes: >\n      Single sifted reference per chromosome consumed by all frequency jobs.\n      Same re-use pattern as mutation_overlap.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:population->mutation_overlap\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:mutation_overlap\n    data_id: data:population_list\n    cardinality: 1_to_1\n    pattern: direct\n    notes: >\n      Each mutation_overlap job reads the population file corresponding to its\n      population parameter. No replication; each population file used only once per\n      (chromosome, population) pair.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:population->frequency\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:frequency\n    data_id: data:population_list\n    cardinality: 1_to_1\n    pattern: direct\n    notes: >\n      Each frequency job reads the population file for its population parameter.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:columns->individuals\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:individuals\n    data_id: data:columns_metadata\n    cardinality: n_to_1\n    pattern: shared_input\n    notes: >\n      Single columns.txt file read by all individuals jobs. Shared input; no duplication.\n      Used to name per-individual output files.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:columns->mutation_overlap\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:mutation_overlap\n    data_id: data:columns_metadata\n    cardinality: n_to_1\n    pattern: shared_input\n    notes: >\n      Single columns.txt file read by all mutation_overlap jobs for alignment\n      with individuals data.\n\n  - pc_edge_id: pc:columns->frequency\n    producer_task: external_input\n    consumer_task: task:frequency\n    data_id: data:columns_metadata\n    cardinality: n_to_1\n    pattern: shared_input\n    notes: >\n      Single columns.txt file read by all frequency jobs.\n\nworkflow_graph:\n  dag_topology: >\n    Two independent pipelines merge at the analysis stage.\n    Pipeline 1 (individuals): vcf_input -> individuals [embarrassingly parallel]\n    -> individuals_merge [reduction] -> analysis tasks.\n    Pipeline 2 (sifting): vep_annotation -> sifting [per chromosome] -> analysis tasks.\n    Analysis stage: Cartesian product of (individuals_merge output) × (sifting output)\n    × (population_list) -> mutation_overlap and frequency (scatter-scatter pattern).\n    External shared inputs (columns, population files) stage-in once, used by multiple\n    tasks.\n\n  control_flow: >\n    Acyclic DAG with two main dependency chains:\n    1. individuals -> individuals_merge -> analysis\n    2. sifting -> analysis\n    Analysis stage (mutation_overlap, frequency) has Cartesian product dependencies:\n    must complete for all (chromosome, population) pairs before workflow finishes.\n    No loops, conditionals, or dynamic generation observed.\n\n  iteration_pattern: \"none\"\n\n  branching_pattern: >\n    Static Cartesian product expansion: once workflow starts, all task instances\n    are known. No dynamic or conditional task generation. Output branching at\n    analysis stage: one chromosome-population pair per job.\n\n  critical_path_estimate: >\n    [ASSUMPTION] Assuming ~10 chromosomes, 1 individuals job per chromosome:\n    individuals (longest, dominated by I/O and VCF parsing) dominates execution time.\n    README indicates ~11,431 seconds for individuals, ~1,492 seconds for frequency.\n    Critical path: vcf_input -> individuals [~11.4K sec per chromosome max] ->\n    individuals_merge [~500 sec] -> mutation_overlap/frequency [~1,500 sec max per population].\n    Parallelism within (individuals chunks across chromosomes) and analysis stage\n    (across populations) can overlap. Total: linear in max(chromosome_count, population_count).\n\nexecution_profiles:\n  - profile_id: \"local\"\n    name: \"Local HTCondor Execution\"\n    description: >\n      Default execution using local HTCondor pool. All tasks run on compute nodes\n      with shared filesystem (condorio data configuration).\n    sites:\n      - execution_site: \"local\"\n        data_config: \"condorio\"\n      - site_catalog_type: \"local\"\n\n  - profile_id: \"cori_slurm\"\n    name: \"NERSC Cori (SLURM)\"\n    description: >\n      HPC cluster execution at NERSC Cori using SLURM scheduler.\n      Pegasus SSH pilot (Bosco) for remote submission.\n      Shared filesystem for data staging.\n    sites:\n      - execution_site: \"cori\"\n        scheduler: \"slurm\"\n        data_config: \"sharedfs\"\n      - site_catalog_type: \"remote_hpc\"\n\n  - profile_id: \"decaf_aggregation\"\n    name: \"In-Memory Data Staging (Decaf)\"\n    description: >\n      Uses Decaf in-memory aggregation for individuals and individuals_merge jobs.\n      Reduces I/O to shared filesystem by staging intermediate data in node memory.\n      Requires MPI and Decaf runtime on compute nodes.\n    conditional_inclusions:\n      - task_id: task:individuals\n        condition: \"use_decaf flag = true\"\n        variant_executable: \"bin/individuals_mpi.py\"\n      - task_id: task:individuals_merge\n        condition: \"use_decaf flag = true\"\n        variant_executable: \"bin/individuals_merge_mpi.py\"\n\n  - profile_id: \"pmc_aggregation\"\n    name: \"MPI Cluster Aggregation (PMC)\"\n    description: >\n      Uses Pegasus MPI Cluster (PMC) mode for individuals and individuals_merge jobs.\n      Aggregates multiple task instances within a single MPI job using leader/follower\n      paradigm. Reduces scheduler overhead and job startup cost.\n    conditional_inclusions:\n      - task_id: task:individuals\n        condition: \"use_pmc flag = true\"\n      - task_id: task:individuals_merge\n        condition: \"use_pmc flag = true\"\n\nworkflow_patterns:\n  - pattern_name: \"map-reduce\"\n    description: >\n      Individuals stage exhibits map-reduce structure:\n      Map: individuals jobs (embarrassingly parallel per chromosome chunk)\n      Reduce: individuals_merge (gather and consolidate).\n      Analysis stage inverts to reduce-scatter:\n      Reduce: single individuals_merge and sifting outputs per chromosome\n      Scatter: Cartesian product across populations.\n\n  - pattern_name: \"data_reuse\"\n    description: >\n      Significant intermediate data reuse: merged individuals archive and sifted\n      reference read by multiple downstream jobs (one per population per chromosome).\n      No duplication; shared read-only access. Enables efficient pipeline composition.\n\n  - pattern_name: \"cartesian_expansion\"\n    description: >\n      Analysis stage exhibits Cartesian product pattern:\n      Task instances = chromosomes × populations. Both mutation_overlap and frequency\n      instantiated identically, enabling independent job generation and scheduling.\n\nio_behavioral_hints:\n  - concern_id: io:vcf_parsing\n    category: \"data_format\"\n    task_id: task:individuals\n    severity: \"moderate\"\n    description: >\n      VCF file parsing by line-by-line text extraction in Python.\n      Each individuals job processes a disjoint range (counter:stop parameters),\n      requiring random-access lookup by line number. Uses Python readfile() to\n      load entire file into memory, then slices range. Inefficient for large files\n      (6-7 GB uncompressed). Could benefit from iterative line reading or indexed VCF.\n    mitigation: >\n      Consider streaming VCF parsing (e.g., vcf library) to reduce memory overhead.\n      Prefetch only the required line range. Index VCF for fast range lookup.\n\n  - concern_id: io:tar_overhead\n    category: \"format_overhead\"\n    task_id: task:individuals_merge\n    severity: \"moderate\"\n    description: >\n      Each individuals chunk archived in tar.gz; individuals_merge extracts all,\n      re-archives. Multiple tar operations (extract → merge → recompress) create\n      redundant I/O and CPU overhead. Especially costly if chunks are many and large.\n    mitigation: >\n      Consider avoiding intermediate archiving; write individuals directly to shared\n      space and let merge aggregate without tar. Or use tar streaming (e.g., tar -cf -\n      to pipe). Evaluate trade-off between temporary storage (flatten) vs. compression overhead.\n\n  - concern_id: io:small_file_overhead\n    category: \"small_file_problem\"\n    task_id: task:individuals\n    severity: \"high\"\n    description: >\n      Individuals task creates one file per individual (2504 total) per chunk.\n      Even with chunking (e.g., 250 chunks), this produces thousands of small files\n      in a single tar archive. Small-file I/O is notoriously inefficient at scale:\n      metadata server overhead, filesystem lookup cost, archive extraction complexity.\n      Typical impact: orders of magnitude slower than large-file sequential I/O.\n    mitigation: >\n      Consolidate per-individual files earlier (e.g., individual ID -> binary pickle\n      or HDF5 dataset). Use HDF5 parallel mode (hdf5_parallel) to write efficiently\n      from multiple jobs. Or buffer small files into larger chunks before writing.\n\n  - concern_id: io:data_reuse_extraction\n    category: \"partial_read\"\n    task_id: task:mutation_overlap\n    severity: \"moderate\"\n    description: >\n      Merged individuals archive is extracted by every mutation_overlap job (once per\n      population). If archive is large (1-5 GB) and population is small (e.g., GBR ~100\n      individuals), most files extracted are unused. Wasteful I/O and storage bandwidth.\n    mitigation: >\n      Avoid full extraction. Use tar selective extraction (tar -xf archive individual_list)\n      or pre-split individuals by population during merge. Or use index-based file formats\n      (HDF5) with per-population datasets to avoid full read.\n\n  - concern_id: io:matplotlib_output\n    category: \"formatting\"\n    task_id: task:mutation_overlap\n    severity: \"low\"\n    description: >\n      Matplotlib PNG generation in mutation_overlap and frequency tasks. PNG encoding\n      is CPU-intensive (especially for large matrices); I/O is sequential (single-threaded).\n      Noticeable but not critical for typical problem sizes.\n    mitigation: >\n      Cache plots in memory before writing (already done). Consider async I/O for\n      multiple plots if scaling to very large populations. Evaluate plot resolution\n      trade-offs (smaller PNG = faster write).\n\n  - concern_id: io:mpi_io_pattern_risk\n    category: \"mpi_io_pattern_risk\"\n    task_id: task:individuals_mpi\n    severity: \"unknown\"\n    description: >\n      MPI variant (individuals_mpi.py) uses mpi4py for parallel processing.\n      Static analysis cannot determine MPI.File or HDF5 collective vs. independent\n      I/O calls. If individuals_merge_mpi.py uses independent I/O on shared tar files,\n      risks metadata server congestion at scale (many ranks writing same archive).\n    mitigation: >\n      Verify MPI-IO usage in individuals_mpi.py and individuals_merge_mpi.py.\n      If independent, consider switching to collective or POSIX with file locking.\n\n  - concern_id: io:no_checkpointing\n    category: \"failure_recovery\"\n    task_id: task:frequency\n    severity: \"moderate\"\n    description: >\n      Frequency task performs 1000 Monte Carlo iterations (n_runs=1000) without\n      intermediate checkpointing. Failure mid-run requires full restart. If each\n      iteration takes minutes, total cost can be high. Not idempotent w.r.t. exact\n      output (though statistically robust).\n    mitigation: >\n      Add checkpoint/resume logic: save iteration results periodically, load on\n      restart. Trade memory/storage for job robustness at scale.\n\n  - concern_id: io:archive_reuse\n    category: \"data_reuse\"\n    task_id: task:mutation_overlap\n    severity: \"beneficial\"\n    description: >\n      Merged individuals archive is read-only, shared across many mutation_overlap\n      jobs. No duplication; each jo